T40.01|Reading an annual report begins with the business and reporting scope, then connects statements, notes, policies and risks. The notes explain composition and judgments that headline totals cannot show. Compare periods and distinguish consolidated results from standalone accounts.~~For a real issuer, use the actual dated report and identify page or note references. The case below is an invented excerpt to practise the method, not a quotation from a company.|Business reading: Products, segments and competitive context.;;Statement reading: Earnings, financial position and cash.;;Note reading: Policies, judgments and detailed balances.|An illustrative report shows revenue ₹120 lakh, profit ₹12, CFO ₹4 and receivables rising ₹10.~~Compare profit with operating cash.;;Inspect credit terms and receivable notes.;;Check working-capital movements and one-time items before concluding.~~The gap raises questions about conversion, not automatic proof of wrongdoing.~~Other noncash and cash items need reconciliation.|Record reporting scope and dates.;;Read relevant notes.;;Build a questions list tied to evidence.
T40.02|Tracing transactions across statements teaches why profit, assets and cash move differently. Revenue earned on credit increases earnings and receivables without an immediate receipt; collection later swaps receivables for cash. Inventory cost becomes expense when the related sale is recognised under the simplified case.~~Maintain the accounting equation after every step and reconcile the cash-flow statement to cash changes.|Accrual event: Recognises earned income or incurred expense.;;Cash event: Receives or pays money.;;Balance-sheet link: Records assets, obligations and retained earnings.|Start with ₹20 lakh cash and equity; buy ₹8 inventory for cash, sell it on credit for ₹12, then collect ₹5.~~After purchase: cash ₹12, inventory ₹8.;;After sale: profit ₹4, receivables ₹12, equity ₹24.;;After collection: cash ₹17 and receivables ₹7.~~Assets ₹24 equal equity ₹24; profit is ₹4 but cash fell ₹3.~~Assume no tax, other costs or beginning balances.|Post each event separately.;;Check the accounting equation.;;Reconcile accrual profit with cash.
T40.03|A profitable company can run short of cash when receivables, inventory, investment or debt repayments absorb funds. Diagnose the timing and source of the shortage before prescribing borrowing. Financing can bridge a temporary gap, but cannot sustainably repair negative underlying economics.~~Separate operating cash, investment and financing. The same reported profit can produce very different funding needs.|Operating gap: Collections lag spending.;;Investment gap: Capex precedes returns.;;Financing gap: Principal or distributions exceed available cash.|Profit is ₹15 lakh, depreciation ₹5 and working capital rises ₹18; capex is ₹10 and debt repayment ₹4.~~Simplified CFO is ₹2.;;Cash before financing falls ₹12 after capex and principal.;;Identify collection, inventory and financing remedies.~~Positive profit did not provide enough cash.~~Tax and other adjustments are assumed already reflected or absent.|Reconcile profit to CFO.;;Classify cash uses.;;Distinguish timing remedies from business-model repairs.
T40.04|Growth creates value when incremental returns exceed the required return on capital, after considering risk and cash timing. Revenue expansion that needs excessive investment can destroy value despite increasing accounting profit. Estimate incremental rather than average economics.~~A profitable existing company can still undertake a poor expansion. Test price, margin, working capital and investment together.|Growth driver: Additional sales or capacity.;;Incremental return: New operating profit relative to new capital.;;Value test: Return compared with required compensation.|Expansion requires ₹50 lakh capital and produces ₹4 annual after-tax operating profit; assumed required return is 10%.~~Incremental return is 8%.;;Required annual capital charge is ₹5.;;Economic profit is −₹1 under this framework.~~Positive profit is insufficient to cover capital's opportunity cost.~~Duration, terminal value and risk require full NPV analysis.|Use incremental cash flows.;;Include working capital and capex.;;Compare returns with an appropriate hurdle.
T40.05|A three-statement forecast links operating assumptions to earnings, assets, obligations and cash. Revenue drives costs and working capital; capex and depreciation connect fixed assets; financing responds to cash needs. The balance sheet should balance because schedules reconcile, not because a hidden plug conceals errors.~~Check economic feasibility as well as arithmetic. A forecast can be internally balanced yet require unavailable borrowing.|Operating schedule: Revenue, margin and costs.;;Balance schedules: Working capital, assets and debt.;;Cash bridge: Explains funding and closing cash.|Forecast sales rise ₹20 lakh at 30% contribution, but incremental working capital is ₹8 and capex ₹5.~~Additional contribution is ₹6 before other effects.;;Investment needs total ₹13.;;Assess a ₹7 preliminary funding gap before tax and financing.~~Growth can increase financing needs despite higher profit.~~Detailed schedules must include all remaining cash effects.|Link assumptions visibly.;;Reconcile cash and debt.;;Test funding feasibility and downside cases.
T40.06|Valuing a listed company combines business understanding, normalised forecasts, required returns and an enterprise-to-equity bridge. Market price is an observable comparison point rather than a model input that must be reproduced. Use ranges reflecting uncertainty rather than false precision.~~Different methods should be reconciled by their assumptions. DCF and peer multiples can disagree because of growth, margins, capital intensity or risk.|DCF: Values future cash flows.;;Relative valuation: Compares market pricing under consistent measures.;;Equity bridge: Adjusts operating value for claims and cash.|Assume operating EV ₹200 lakh, debt ₹60, excess cash ₹20 and 10 lakh diluted shares.~~Equity value is ₹160.;;Value per share is ₹16.;;Compare with a hypothetical ₹20 market price and stress inputs.~~The model implies downside on these assumptions, not a certain future price.~~Debt-like claims and dilution may change the bridge.|Normalise forecasts.;;Use consistent enterprise and equity metrics.;;Report sensitivities and evidence.
T40.07|An equity research report communicates the business thesis, valuation, catalysts, risks and evidence. It should distinguish facts from forecasts and explain what would invalidate the thesis. A recommendation without assumptions is difficult to review or update.~~Price targets are conditional estimates. Track thesis development and risk rather than attributing every short-term price move to research quality.|Thesis: Why the market may misprice future economics.;;Valuation: Supported range and assumptions.;;Monitoring: Evidence, catalysts and invalidation conditions.|A thesis assumes revenue growth 12%, margin 15% and receivable days below 60.~~State the source and rationale for each assumption.;;Define a review if margins fall to 10% or collections weaken.;;Link valuation sensitivity to those changes.~~A testable thesis supports disciplined updates.~~Thresholds here are illustrative rather than universal rules.|Separate facts and estimates.;;Disclose risks and conflicts.;;Set evidence-based review conditions.
T40.08|Corporate loan underwriting evaluates how a borrower will repay, what can go wrong and what protections exist. Cash coverage, leverage, business risk and collateral work together. Collateral is a secondary recovery source rather than a substitute for viable operating repayment.~~Match facilities to the purpose and cash cycle. Seasonal working-capital needs differ from permanent investment funding.|Repayment source: Operating cash or defined proceeds.;;Structure: Tenor, amortisation and covenants.;;Recovery support: Security and claim priority.|Borrower CFADS is ₹18 lakh and proposed annual debt service ₹12.~~Base coverage is 1.5×.;;A 30% cash decline gives ₹12.6 and coverage 1.05×.;;Assess other debt, liquidity and recoveries.~~Base-case coverage can conceal thin downside headroom.~~Use the lender's precise CFADS and service definitions.|Verify cash generation.;;Stress coverage and refinancing.;;Review security and enforceable covenants.
T40.09|A capex proposal compares incremental investment and future cash benefits with alternatives, including doing nothing. Include working capital, opportunity costs, taxes and end-of-life effects. Sunk costs should not determine whether additional investment is worthwhile.~~Separate demand certainty from engineering feasibility. Positive accounting savings can still yield a negative NPV if the investment is too large.|Incremental case: Cash changes caused by the project.;;Alternative case: Competing uses or no investment.;;Risk case: Delays, overruns and lower benefits.|A machine costs ₹20 lakh today and saves ₹8 annually for three years; assume 10% discount rate and no residual value.~~PV of savings is about ₹19.89.;;NPV is about −₹0.11.;;Test lower cost, longer useful life and downside savings transparently.~~Simple payback below three years does not guarantee positive NPV.~~Tax and working capital are excluded here.|Use incremental dated cash.;;Compare alternatives.;;Test key risks and investment limits.
T40.10|Debt-versus-equity decisions balance funding cost, risk, ownership and flexibility. Debt imposes contractual payments; equity shares residual outcomes and dilutes ownership. A low debt rate cannot justify borrowing beyond sustainable cash capacity.~~Consider adverse conditions and future funding needs. The best capital structure depends on business resilience and objectives rather than a universal debt percentage.|Debt: Fixed claims and payment obligations.;;Equity: Residual risk and ownership sharing.;;Hybrid: Combines selected debt and equity features.|A company needs ₹50 lakh; debt would cost 10% and require ₹10 yearly principal, while forecast CFADS is ₹12.~~First-year interest is ₹5.;;Debt service ₹15 exceeds forecast cash by ₹3.;;Compare a mixed or equity structure and revised terms.~~Debt can be cheaper yet unaffordable.~~Dilution and valuation need a separate ownership model.|Test debt service under stress.;;Model dilution and flexibility.;;Include covenants and future capital needs.
T40.11|Acquisition evaluation combines strategic rationale, standalone value, synergies, financing and integration. An EPS-accretive model is one output rather than the final value test. The buyer should assess who captures benefits and how execution risk changes the outcome.~~Use a clear no-deal comparison. Avoid embedding synergies in both the target forecast and a separate addition.|Standalone case: Target without buyer-specific changes.;;Combined case: Includes incremental benefits and costs.;;Funding/integration case: Tests implementation and resilience.|Target standalone value is ₹80 lakh, synergy PV ₹25 and integration cost PV ₹10; price is ₹100.~~Net justified value is ₹95.;;Buyer NPV is −₹5 on these inputs.;;Test price and evidence for additional benefits.~~An attractive strategic narrative does not overcome the arithmetic.~~Uncertainty may widen the downside further.|Compare with no deal.;;Avoid double-counting synergies.;;Stress financing and integration.
T40.12|Startup financing should connect runway, milestones, pricing and the full ownership waterfall. New primary equity adds company cash while reducing existing holders' percentage; option pools and convertibles can add dilution. Valuation must be considered with rights and future funding needs.~~A round that funds too little may force another raise before progress supports better terms. Plan a contingency rather than assuming continuous investor appetite.|Funding requirement: Cash to milestones plus contingencies.;;Cap table: Ownership under defined dilution assumptions.;;Exit rights: Preferences and conversion outcomes.|A startup raises ₹20 lakh at ₹60 pre-money, with no other securities; monthly net burn is ₹4 and existing cash ₹8.~~New investor owns 25% of ₹80 post-money.;;Cash becomes ₹28, giving seven months constant-burn runway.;;Assess milestones and fundraising lead time.~~Ownership and runway answer different questions about the same round.~~Fees and changing burn reduce simple runway.|Model all securities.;;Tie proceeds to milestones.;;Test down-round and exit outcomes.
T40.13|Property investment analysis combines purchase costs, rent, vacancy, operating costs, capex, financing and exit value. Cap rates, DSCR and equity IRR answer different questions and should be reconciled. A high rental yield can conceal renewal spending or a weak exit assumption.~~Use dated cash flows and stress both income and valuation. Do not assume refinancing is available at the desired time.|Asset analysis: NOI and capital requirements.;;Debt analysis: Service and refinancing.;;Equity analysis: Distributions and residual sale proceeds.|Buy for ₹100 lakh with debt ₹60 at 10%, no amortisation; NOI ₹9 and recurring capex ₹2.~~Interest is ₹6.;;Equity cash is ₹1 before tax.;;On ₹40 equity, cash yield is 2.5%, despite 9% NOI yield.~~Asset yield is not equity distributable yield.~~Sale value and principal repayment affect total return.|Include vacancy and capex.;;Reconcile debt service.;;Stress exit rates and cash access.
T40.14|Portfolio construction connects objectives, constraints, allocation, instrument selection and monitoring. Evaluate the aggregate exposure and liability fit, not just individual product attractiveness. Rebalancing rules should consider taxes, costs and changed circumstances.~~An allocation is a conditional plan rather than a guaranteed outcome. Stress how losses affect essential goals and cash needs.|Policy: Objectives and permitted risk.;;Implementation: Holdings, costs and liquidity.;;Review: Rebalancing and goal changes.|A ₹100 lakh portfolio holds ₹70 growth assets and ₹30 liquid assets; ₹25 is needed in one year.~~Identify the near-term cash liability.;;Stress a 30% fall in growth assets, producing ₹21 loss.;;Assess whether liquid assets remain available and sufficient.~~Goal funding and portfolio return must be assessed together.~~The allocation is illustrative, not advice.|Start with liabilities and horizon.;;Look through overlapping holdings.;;Define review and rebalancing rules.
T40.15|Stress testing a bank or business connects operating, credit, market and funding shocks to earnings, capital and cash. Scenarios should be coherent and include feedback such as forced asset sales or reduced lending. Passing one scenario does not establish universal resilience.~~Management actions must be realistic, timed and permitted. Assuming instant new equity can conceal the very risk being tested.|Earnings stress: Lower income or higher losses.;;Capital stress: Losses reduce eligible resources.;;Liquidity stress: Payments and withdrawals exceed accessible funds.|A simplified bank has ₹100 lakh capital and ₹30 liquid assets; stress causes ₹25 credit loss and ₹40 deposit outflow.~~Capital falls to ₹75 before other effects.;;Liquid assets cover only ₹30 of the ₹40 outflow.;;Assess funding or asset-sale actions and their costs.~~Capital survival does not guarantee liquidity survival.~~Regulatory requirements and asset eligibility require separate analysis.|Stress capital and cash separately.;;Include correlated shocks.;;Validate contingency actions and timing.
T40.16|Analysing accounting failures examines incentives, transactions, reporting judgments and controls. Warning signs can include weak cash conversion, opaque related parties, unusual recognition or unsupported assets. They are investigation prompts rather than proof of fraud.~~Use primary records and distinguish allegation, restatement and established findings in real cases. Reconcile economic events before inferring intent.|Recognition risk: Income recorded before criteria are met.;;Asset risk: Balances lack recoverable support.;;Control risk: Conflicts or verification failures.|A hypothetical company reports ₹20 lakh profit and ₹2 CFO, with receivables growing ₹18 and several related-party sales.~~Reconcile noncash and working-capital items.;;Inspect sale terms, collections and counterparties.;;Seek independent evidence before drawing conclusions.~~The pattern warrants scrutiny but has possible legitimate explanations.~~Intent and legal findings require evidence.|Trace transactions and cash.;;Review incentives and related parties.;;Separate red flags from proven findings.
T40.17|Studying crises, bubbles and collapses identifies mechanisms such as leverage, maturity mismatch, common exposure and confidence loss. Historical cases should use primary records and distinguish what participants knew then from later knowledge. A narrative alone can oversimplify interacting causes.~~Lessons should test current vulnerabilities without assuming every crisis repeats identically. Feedback loops often matter more than one initial trigger.|Trigger: Initial shock or new information.;;Amplifier: Leverage, runs or forced sales.;;Propagation: Transmission through markets and institutions.|In a hypothetical system, a 10% asset decline triggers collateral calls, forcing sales that cause another 10% decline.~~Two sequential declines reduce value 19%, not 20%.;;Trace financing and affected counterparties.;;Assess buffers that could interrupt the loop.~~Feedback can turn a manageable shock into wider failure.~~Real historical attribution requires case-specific evidence.|Reconstruct dated information.;;Map triggers and amplifiers.;;Identify practical resilience lessons.
T40.18|Comparing a thesis with outcomes distinguishes forecasting error, implementation error, changed conditions and random variation. A profitable investment can result from a weak process, and a good process can lose. Review the original assumptions rather than rewriting them after the event.~~Separate valuation changes from operating performance and currency or financing effects. Use the review to improve future decisions and calibration.|Thesis review: Compares original drivers with evidence.;;Attribution: Explains sources of realised return.;;Process review: Evaluates decisions with information then available.|A share was bought for ₹100 expecting EPS ₹10 and P/E 12; actual EPS is ₹8 and market P/E 15, giving price ₹120.~~Forecast target was ₹120.;;Actual price also reaches ₹120 through different drivers.;;Review the earnings miss and multiple expansion separately.~~A correct price outcome does not validate the original reasoning.~~Dividends, timing and costs are excluded.|Preserve the original thesis.;;Attribute outcomes by driver.;;Update process and probabilities without hindsight editing.
